INT. PHOTOGRAPHER'S STUDIO - DARKROOM - NIGHT - 1893

Red lamp. Trays of chemicals. Glass plates drying on a rack. The air smells of vinegar.

ELIZA PRYCE (30s, long sleeves rolled, stained gloves) lifts a plate from the developing bath with tongs. JASPER (12, apprentice, oversized apron) stands on a stool holding a lantern shaded in red tissue. MRS. WHITLOCK (50s, widow's black, a hat pinned too firmly) waits at the door, clutching a reticule.

ELIZA
Jasper, hold the light steadier.

JASPER
Yes, Miss Pryce.

ELIZA
Mrs. Whitlock, you may come closer. But not past the line.

MRS. WHITLOCK
What line?

JASPER
The chalk, ma'am.

Mrs. Whitlock steps up to the chalk mark. The plate drips. In the red light, a ghostly negative: a woman at a window, and over her shoulder a faint pale shape.

MRS. WHITLOCK
Is that...

ELIZA
Your husband, I believe.

MRS. WHITLOCK
He's been dead eleven years.

ELIZA
Then he's very patient.

JASPER
Miss Pryce, do you want it fixed?

ELIZA
Yes, Jasper. Fix it before the lady changes her mind.

Mrs. Whitlock watches the plate slide into the fixing bath. The shape stays.
